# Build-context exclusions for the images built from the REPOSITORY ROOT:
#   ingestion/Dockerfile            -> ingestion daemon
#   supabase/functions/Dockerfile   -> edge runtime (needs gateway-bundle-template)
#   test-harness/Dockerfile         -> conformance test runner
#   i3x/Dockerfile                  -> i3X server (compiles sparkplug_b.proto)
#   swagger-ui/Dockerfile           -> the API documentation site (needs both docs/*.yaml)
#
# ONE .dockerignore SERVES THEM ALL, so an exclusion added for one image can break another. That is
# not hypothetical: excluding `test-harness` for the ingestion build broke the test-runner build,
# which needs test-harness/schemas, and it failed as a "not found" during COPY, which reads like a
# missing file rather than an ignored one.
#
# frontend/ has its own .dockerignore because it is built from its own context. It must keep
# frontend/src/utils/ -- the test runner reads sparkplugDatatype.js and model3d.js from THIS context
# for its drift checks against the edge worker's copies.

.git
.github
node_modules
**/node_modules
frontend/dist
frontend/coverage

# Local secrets. .env.example is deliberately NOT excluded -- it is the documented template.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Nothing in these is read at build time by the images above; excluding them keeps the context small
# and stops an unrelated edit from busting the layer cache.
#
# `test-harness` is NOT excluded: test-harness/Dockerfile copies test-harness/schemas, the vendored
# IDTA metamodel schema. Its absence would make the AAS suite skip its schema-conformance
# assertions while still reporting success.
#
# BOTH SPECS UNDER `docs` ARE NEGATED, and each is needed by a different image:
#   openapi.yaml      test-harness/Dockerfile -- test_aas_api.py asserts that every route the
#                     service advertises is documented, so the spec is a fixture of the suite.
#   i3x-openapi.yaml  swagger-ui/Dockerfile, which serves both documents.
# Without a negation the build fails as `"/docs/<name>": not found`, which reads like a deleted
# file rather than an ignored one.
#
# The negations have to come AFTER the `docs` line: Docker applies these in order and the last match
# wins. Putting them before makes them no-ops that look correct.
docs
!docs/openapi.yaml
!docs/i3x-openapi.yaml
grafana
simulators
timescaledb
deploy
supabase/migrations/archive

*.md
!supabase/functions/**/*.md
